1
00:00:01,240 --> 00:00:03,114
Right now, sitting perfectly still,

2
00:00:03,134 --> 00:00:03,989
you are moving.

3
00:00:04,009 --> 00:00:04,629
Fast.

4
00:00:05,399 --> 00:00:06,719
For most people on Earth,

5
00:00:06,939 --> 00:00:10,025
the ground under your feet is swinging around the planet's

6
00:00:10,045 --> 00:00:13,003
axis at more than a thousand kilometres an hour.

7
00:00:13,433 --> 00:00:14,263
At the equator,

8
00:00:14,283 --> 00:00:16,403
it's about sixteen hundred and seventy.

9
00:00:16,673 --> 00:00:18,602
That's faster than the speed of sound.

10
00:00:19,352 --> 00:00:20,467
You don't feel a thing.

11
00:00:20,487 --> 00:00:21,517
But what if, one day,

12
00:00:21,537 --> 00:00:22,816
Earth just stopped?

13
00:00:23,966 --> 00:00:25,136
No more spin.

14
00:00:25,436 --> 00:00:27,166
The winds, the oceans,

15
00:00:27,196 --> 00:00:28,802
even the length of a day would change

16
00:00:28,822 --> 00:00:30,587
in ways that are hard to believe.

17
00:00:31,337 --> 00:00:33,692
So, what would happen if Earth stopped spinning?

18
00:00:39,422 --> 00:00:40,282
Chapter one.

19
00:00:40,302 --> 00:00:41,797
The ride you never feel.

20
00:00:42,477 --> 00:00:44,897
If we're all moving faster than a jet plane,

21
00:00:45,047 --> 00:00:47,347
why does everything feel perfectly still?

22
00:00:47,777 --> 00:00:49,347
Earth turns once a day.

23
00:00:49,617 --> 00:00:50,577
To be exact,

24
00:00:50,597 --> 00:00:53,427
once every twenty three hours and fifty six minutes,

25
00:00:53,457 --> 00:00:55,080
measured against the stars.

26
00:00:55,510 --> 00:00:56,390
In that time,

27
00:00:56,410 --> 00:00:59,450
a spot on the equator travels all the way around,

28
00:00:59,600 --> 00:01:01,580
about forty thousand kilometres.

29
00:01:01,910 --> 00:01:05,309
That's roughly sixteen hundred and seventy kilometres an hour.

30
00:01:06,069 --> 00:01:08,559
Move toward the poles and your circle gets smaller,

31
00:01:08,579 --> 00:01:09,769
so you go slower.

32
00:01:10,099 --> 00:01:12,589
Baku, about twelve hundred and seventy.

33
00:01:12,869 --> 00:01:15,209
London, about a thousand and forty.

34
00:01:15,359 --> 00:01:16,739
Stand on the North Pole,

35
00:01:16,759 --> 00:01:18,547
and you just turn on the spot.

36
00:01:19,387 --> 00:01:21,437
The spin even changes your weight.

37
00:01:21,767 --> 00:01:24,687
On the equator,

38
00:01:24,837 --> 00:01:27,307
it flings you outward just a little,

39
00:01:27,327 --> 00:01:28,207
so you weigh about a third of a percent

40
00:01:28,227 --> 00:01:29,581
less than you would if the planet stood still.

41
00:01:30,321 --> 00:01:31,601
So why don't we feel it?

42
00:01:31,771 --> 00:01:34,661
Because your body only feels changes in speed.

43
00:01:34,931 --> 00:01:36,001
On a smooth flight,

44
00:01:36,111 --> 00:01:37,277
you can pour a drink at

45
00:01:37,297 --> 00:01:39,107
nine hundred kilometres an hour.

46
00:01:39,837 --> 00:01:42,036
Earth is the smoothest ride there is.

47
00:01:42,056 --> 00:01:44,357
The ground, the air, the oceans, and you,

48
00:01:44,377 --> 00:01:45,795
all move together.

49
00:01:46,235 --> 00:01:48,325
The trouble starts if one of them stops,

50
00:01:48,345 --> 00:01:49,539
and the others don't.

51
00:01:51,309 --> 00:01:52,129
Chapter two.

52
00:01:52,149 --> 00:01:53,309
The sudden stop.

53
00:01:53,989 --> 00:01:56,489
Imagine the planet hits the brakes in an instant.

54
00:01:56,699 --> 00:01:57,779
The ground stops.

55
00:01:57,799 --> 00:01:58,561
You don't.

56
00:01:58,991 --> 00:02:02,101
Everything that isn't bolted to bedrock keeps going east,

57
00:02:02,211 --> 00:02:05,131
at up to sixteen hundred and seventy kilometres an hour.

58
00:02:05,481 --> 00:02:08,363
People, cars, trees, loose rock.

59
00:02:09,193 --> 00:02:10,773
The air keeps going too.

60
00:02:11,113 --> 00:02:13,093
A wind more than three times faster

61
00:02:13,113 --> 00:02:15,873
than the strongest tornado winds ever measured,

62
00:02:16,013 --> 00:02:18,373
sweeping across the whole planet at once.

63
00:02:19,113 --> 00:02:20,888
And the oceans would slosh out of

64
00:02:20,908 --> 00:02:23,505
their basins and surge across the continents.

65
00:02:23,935 --> 00:02:27,325
Buildings ripped from their foundations, forests flattened,

66
00:02:27,495 --> 00:02:29,520
the land swept clean of almost anything

67
00:02:29,540 --> 00:02:31,685
that isn't part of the rock itself.

68
00:02:32,145 --> 00:02:33,386
Now, the honest part.

69
00:02:33,406 --> 00:02:34,962
This can't actually happen.

70
00:02:34,982 --> 00:02:37,326
Earth's spin holds about two times ten

71
00:02:37,346 --> 00:02:39,951
to the twenty nine joules of energy.

72
00:02:40,781 --> 00:02:43,094
That's more than three hundred million years

73
00:02:43,114 --> 00:02:45,101
of all the energy humanity uses.

74
00:02:45,391 --> 00:02:48,191
Nothing we know of could stop that in an instant.

75
00:02:48,931 --> 00:02:50,881
So let's take the gentler version.

76
00:02:51,121 --> 00:02:53,041
The spin slowly winds down,

77
00:02:53,061 --> 00:02:54,351
all the way to zero.

78
00:02:54,631 --> 00:02:57,482
Even then, our planet would be unrecognisable.

79
00:02:59,262 --> 00:03:00,182
Chapter three.

80
00:03:00,202 --> 00:03:01,637
The oceans move out.

81
00:03:02,317 --> 00:03:03,417
Without the spin,

82
00:03:03,507 --> 00:03:05,993
the oceans would pack up and leave the equator.

83
00:03:06,423 --> 00:03:08,433
Spinning has squashed our planet.

84
00:03:08,763 --> 00:03:11,451
Earth is about forty three kilometres wider at the

85
00:03:11,471 --> 00:03:13,832
equator than it is from pole to pole.

86
00:03:14,272 --> 00:03:16,572
The spin flings water outward too.

87
00:03:16,872 --> 00:03:18,267
Today the oceans are piled up

88
00:03:18,287 --> 00:03:19,912
in a bulge around the middle,

89
00:03:20,002 --> 00:03:21,714
about eight kilometres high.

90
00:03:22,464 --> 00:03:23,564
Stop the spin,

91
00:03:23,614 --> 00:03:25,304
and all that water flows downhill,

92
00:03:25,324 --> 00:03:26,440
toward the poles.

93
00:03:27,380 --> 00:03:31,050
Geographer Witold Fraczek modelled this with mapping software.

94
00:03:31,460 --> 00:03:33,730
The result: two great oceans,

95
00:03:33,750 --> 00:03:35,090
one around each pole,

96
00:03:35,190 --> 00:03:36,821
and a single giant continent wrapped

97
00:03:36,841 --> 00:03:38,641
around the middle of the planet.

98
00:03:39,681 --> 00:03:41,331
Measured from the new sea level,

99
00:03:41,411 --> 00:03:44,441
the Andes would rise more than twelve kilometres high.

100
00:03:44,801 --> 00:03:46,591
Your favourite beach would be a long,

101
00:03:46,611 --> 00:03:48,147
long way from the sea.

102
00:03:48,577 --> 00:03:50,451
A few deep basins would keep

103
00:03:50,471 --> 00:03:52,507
some water as isolated seas.

104
00:03:52,797 --> 00:03:54,422
And the two polar oceans wouldn't

105
00:03:54,442 --> 00:03:56,127
even sit at the same height.

106
00:03:56,457 --> 00:04:00,274
Their surfaces would differ by about one point four kilometres.

107
00:04:02,054 --> 00:04:03,014
Chapter four.

108
00:04:03,034 --> 00:04:04,557
A day that lasts a year.

109
00:04:05,227 --> 00:04:06,557
If Earth stopped turning,

110
00:04:06,637 --> 00:04:09,019
your next sunrise could be six months away.

111
00:04:09,449 --> 00:04:11,209
We'd still race around the Sun,

112
00:04:11,299 --> 00:04:14,429
at about a hundred and seven thousand kilometres an hour.

113
00:04:14,849 --> 00:04:16,049
But with no spin,

114
00:04:16,139 --> 00:04:18,679
each place would face the Sun for half the year,

115
00:04:18,769 --> 00:04:20,677
and face away for the other half.

116
00:04:21,507 --> 00:04:22,917
Six months of daylight.

117
00:04:23,117 --> 00:04:24,357
The day side would bake,

118
00:04:24,377 --> 00:04:26,186
with no night to cool it down.

119
00:04:26,626 --> 00:04:27,516
Six months of darkness.

120
00:04:27,536 --> 00:04:31,316
The night side would sink into a long, deep freeze.

121
00:04:32,146 --> 00:04:34,353
Every plant and animal tuned to a twenty

122
00:04:34,373 --> 00:04:36,761
four hour rhythm would suddenly be living on

123
00:04:36,781 --> 00:04:39,193
a calendar where one day is one year.

124
00:04:39,613 --> 00:04:41,543
The weather would change completely.

125
00:04:41,813 --> 00:04:44,953
Today, the spin bends moving air sideways.

126
00:04:45,173 --> 00:04:47,073
It's called the Coriolis effect,

127
00:04:47,093 --> 00:04:49,251
and it's what makes hurricanes rotate.

128
00:04:49,681 --> 00:04:51,564
Without it, no spinning storms.

129
00:04:51,584 --> 00:04:53,141
Models suggest one giant,

130
00:04:53,161 --> 00:04:54,283
slow flow of air,

131
00:04:54,303 --> 00:04:56,983
from the hot side to the cold side.

132
00:04:57,713 --> 00:05:02,213
And deep below us,

133
00:05:02,283 --> 00:05:03,176
the spin helps organise the swirling liquid

134
00:05:03,196 --> 00:05:04,233
iron that makes Earth's magnetic field.

135
00:05:04,633 --> 00:05:08,109
Many scientists think that shield would weaken dramatically.

136
00:05:09,889 --> 00:05:10,869
Chapter five.

137
00:05:10,889 --> 00:05:12,413
It's already slowing down.

138
00:05:13,113 --> 00:05:14,183
Here's the twist.

139
00:05:14,263 --> 00:05:16,293
Earth's spin really is winding down.

140
00:05:16,343 --> 00:05:17,239
Right now.

141
00:05:17,659 --> 00:05:20,759
The Moon pulls on our oceans and raises tides.

142
00:05:21,089 --> 00:05:23,729
As the planet turns under those tidal bulges,

143
00:05:23,819 --> 00:05:25,867
friction works like a gentle brake.

144
00:05:26,297 --> 00:05:27,237
On average,

145
00:05:27,327 --> 00:05:28,841
a day gets longer by about

146
00:05:28,861 --> 00:05:31,488
one point eight milliseconds every century.

147
00:05:32,228 --> 00:05:38,218
Tiny, but it adds up.

148
00:05:38,258 --> 00:05:39,029
Fossil corals from about four hundred million

149
00:05:39,049 --> 00:05:39,701
years ago have growth lines that show

150
00:05:39,721 --> 00:05:40,598
around four hundred days in a year.

151
00:05:41,018 --> 00:05:44,107
Back then, a day lasted about twenty two hours.

152
00:05:44,947 --> 00:05:47,117
The spin we lose doesn't just vanish.

153
00:05:47,377 --> 00:05:48,697
It's handed to the Moon,

154
00:05:48,717 --> 00:05:50,412
which drifts away from us by about

155
00:05:50,432 --> 00:05:52,437
three point eight centimetres a year.

156
00:05:52,837 --> 00:05:54,967
Astronauts left mirrors on the Moon,

157
00:05:54,987 --> 00:05:56,699
and lasers still measure it.

158
00:05:57,539 --> 00:05:59,239
The spin also wobbles.

159
00:05:59,549 --> 00:06:00,849
On the fifth of July,

160
00:06:00,869 --> 00:06:02,059
twenty twenty four,

161
00:06:02,169 --> 00:06:05,019
Earth set its record for the shortest day ever measured,

162
00:06:05,179 --> 00:06:07,754
one point six six milliseconds short.

163
00:06:08,204 --> 00:06:09,734
Since nineteen seventy two,

164
00:06:09,814 --> 00:06:12,427
timekeepers have added twenty seven leap seconds to

165
00:06:12,447 --> 00:06:14,804
keep our clocks in step with the planet.

166
00:06:15,234 --> 00:06:18,473
This autumn, they voted to retire the leap second.

167
00:06:20,253 --> 00:06:21,213
Chapter six.

168
00:06:21,233 --> 00:06:22,735
Worlds that hit the brakes.

169
00:06:23,415 --> 00:06:26,205
Some worlds really did slow almost to a stop.

170
00:06:26,405 --> 00:06:28,371
One of them is our closest neighbour.

171
00:06:28,811 --> 00:06:31,491
Venus turns so slowly, and backwards,

172
00:06:31,511 --> 00:06:33,098
that one spin takes two hundred

173
00:06:33,118 --> 00:06:34,721
and forty three Earth days.

174
00:06:35,071 --> 00:06:36,651
That's longer than its whole year,

175
00:06:36,671 --> 00:06:38,429
of two hundred and twenty five.

176
00:06:39,199 --> 00:06:41,699
From one sunrise to the next on Venus,

177
00:06:41,829 --> 00:06:44,520
about a hundred and seventeen Earth days go by.

178
00:06:44,980 --> 00:06:46,860
Mercury is stranger still.

179
00:06:47,170 --> 00:06:48,713
It spins three times for every

180
00:06:48,733 --> 00:06:50,250
two trips around the Sun,

181
00:06:50,290 --> 00:06:51,690
so one Mercury day,

182
00:06:51,720 --> 00:06:53,260
sunrise to sunrise,

183
00:06:53,290 --> 00:06:54,855
lasts two of its years.

184
00:06:55,685 --> 00:06:59,065
Venus also has no global magnetic field of its own,

185
00:06:59,255 --> 00:07:02,113
though scientists still debate exactly why.

186
00:07:02,563 --> 00:07:04,123
Our Moon has stopped spinning,

187
00:07:04,143 --> 00:07:05,563
at least as seen from here.

188
00:07:05,913 --> 00:07:07,273
Earth's pull locked it,

189
00:07:07,293 --> 00:07:09,462
so it always shows us the same face.

190
00:07:09,892 --> 00:07:11,762
Could Earth end up locked to the Moon?

191
00:07:11,992 --> 00:07:13,372
In theory, yes,

192
00:07:13,392 --> 00:07:15,812
but only after tens of billions of years.

193
00:07:16,132 --> 00:07:17,052
Long before that,

194
00:07:17,072 --> 00:07:18,912
in about five billion years,

195
00:07:18,972 --> 00:07:21,483
the Sun will swell into a red giant.

196
00:07:22,733 --> 00:07:24,639
So, what if Earth stopped spinning?

197
00:07:25,069 --> 00:07:27,599
A sudden stop would scour the planet clean.

198
00:07:27,919 --> 00:07:30,449
A slow one would move the oceans to the poles,

199
00:07:30,509 --> 00:07:32,139
stretch a day into a year,

200
00:07:32,219 --> 00:07:34,740
and swap our storms for one long wind.

201
00:07:35,580 --> 00:07:37,660
Spin gives us more than sunrise.

202
00:07:37,910 --> 00:07:39,320
It shapes the oceans,

203
00:07:39,340 --> 00:07:40,320
steers the weather,

204
00:07:40,340 --> 00:07:42,745
and helps power the shield around our world.

205
00:07:43,475 --> 00:07:44,685
And it's still turning,

206
00:07:44,705 --> 00:07:45,356
carrying most of us more than

207
00:07:45,376 --> 00:07:46,185
a thousand kilometres every hour.

208
00:07:46,205 --> 00:07:50,395
So smoothly, you never noticed.

209
00:07:50,545 --> 00:07:51,567
Until now.

210
00:07:52,897 --> 00:07:54,647
If you enjoyed this,

211
00:07:54,667 --> 00:07:56,237
give it a like,

212
00:07:56,517 --> 00:07:57,897
and subscribe to Whyverse.

213
00:07:57,977 --> 00:07:59,667
And for shorter stories,

214
00:07:59,687 --> 00:08:00,141
find us on Instagram and TikTok.

